// Map<K, V> -- an associative container, in Prismio.
//
// REQUIREMENTS 13 asked for `Map<K,V>` and a growable `Vec<T>`. Only one of
// those was missing: `List<T>` **is** the growable vector -- runtime/lang_runtime.c
// stores it as a `void**` that doubles on push, and its own comment calls
// `list_new_with_capacity` "Vec::with_capacity". This is the half that did not
// exist.
//
// **A hash table since 2026-08-29, and the previous version of this header is
// worth keeping in view because it named its own fix.** It read:
//
// > Association list, not a hash table, and the reason is a missing language
// > feature rather than a shortcut. Hashing `K` needs a hash function per key
// > type, and dispatching to it needs bounded type parameters -- traits. […]
// > Give it bounds and this file becomes a hash table without its callers
// > changing.
//
// That is exactly what happened. `K: Key + Copy` uses `Key` (std/key.psm) for
// `hash`/`eq` and `Copy` (std/copy.psm) for storage; `mapIndexOf` went from a
// linear scan to a probe, and **no caller
// changed** -- `tests/test_65_map.psm` is untouched and still passes.
//
// **String keys work now.** The old header said they "do not instantiate, and
// that is the language being consistent rather than this module being weak":
// `String ==` compares pointers and is rejected, so a generic `K` could only be
// compared with the operator, so only scalars instantiated. `Key` replaces the
// operator with `eq`, and `impl Key for String` uses `strEquals`. Struct keys
// work too, for any struct that implements `Key`.
//
// **The representation**: two dense parallel arrays in insertion order, plus an
// open-addressed `slots` table of indices into them. Insertion order is not an
// accident to be preserved -- `mapKeyAt`/`mapValueAt` are the iteration API and
// their contract says insertion order, so the entries have to live somewhere
// that is not the probe table.
//
// **Keys may be owned; values may not, and that is a measured limit rather than
// an oversight.** `K: Key + Copy` duplicates a `String` or a struct key into
// the table and `Map<String, Int>` is `--verify` clean at
// 9 allocated / 9 released / 0 leaked. `V` has no bound, so an owned `V` is
// rejected at compile time with "cannot move out of borrowed value `value`" --
// the same honest error the association list gave, and not a silent one.
//
// Bounding `V: Copy` as well was tried and reverted on 2026-08-29: it compiles
// and computes the right answers, and `Map<String, String>` then **leaks every
// value it holds** -- 4 of 14 allocations, rising to 8 of 18 when the lookups
// also answer with copies. The values list is returned from by `mapGetOr`,
// `mapGet` and `mapValueAt`, the analysis sees it escape, and it declines to
// release the container. That is the pass-through escape KNOWN_ISSUES.md tracks, not
// something this file can fix, and a standard-library container that leaks its
// contents is worse than one that says it cannot hold them.
//
// **Triangular probing, no tombstones, and no deletion.** There is no `mapRemove` in
// this API, so no slot ever becomes free after being used, and the probe can stop
// at the first empty slot. Adding deletion means adding tombstones and revisiting
// every loop here; it is not a two-line change and it is not in v0.1.
//
// Integer keys were never a consolation prize for the caller this was written
// for: runtime/aif_support.c interns every name, type and site to an `int` id
// and keys its tables on those.
//
// **That used to be the argument for an avalanche hash and it is the argument
// against one.** The header here read "consecutive small ids with an identity
// hash are one long probe chain", which is true of *linear* probing and false
// of this table: triangular offsets and a power-of-two mask place consecutive
// ids in consecutive buckets, one probe each, and measured they cost **1.00**
// probes per lookup where MurmurHash3's finalizer costs 1.21 — because 1.21 is
// what random placement costs and a dense key set does better than random.
// Scattering them also throws away the prefetching that makes a scan of an
// id-keyed table fast: 7.25 ms against 3.64 ms on `key_value_update`'s update
// phase. `keyMixInt` folds the high half into the low half and stops, and
// `mapRehash` below is what makes that safe.

import std.key
import std.copy
import std.option

// `slots` holds an index into `keys`/`values`, or -1 for empty. Its length is
// always a power of two, so `hash & mask` is the bucket.
struct Map<K: Key + Copy, V> {
    keys: List<K>,
    values: List<V>,
    slots: List<Int>,
    mask: Int,
    // Whether this map has measured its own probe displacement and found the
    // hashes it is being given bad enough to be worth re-mixing. See mapRehash.
    strong: Bool
}

// 8 buckets, which is 4 entries before the first rehash. Small enough that a map
// used as a scratch lookup costs almost nothing, and a power of two so the mask
// is right from the start.
private fn mapInitialCapacity() -> Int { return 8 }

public fn mapNew<K: Key + Copy, V>() -> Map<K, V> {
    let k: List<K> = list_new()
    let v: List<V> = list_new()
    let s: List<Int> = list_new_with_capacity(mapInitialCapacity())
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < mapInitialCapacity()) {
        list_push(s, -1)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return Map<K, V> { keys: k, values: v, slots: s, mask: mapInitialCapacity() - 1,
                       strong: false }
}

public fn mapLen<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>) -> Int {
    return list_len(m.keys)
}

// **Both modes answer a hit with the dense entry index.** They differ only in
// what a miss is: `findSlot` reports the empty bucket it stopped on, encoded as
// `-bucket - 2`, and a lookup reports -1.
//
// Encoding the bucket rather than returning it plainly is what removes the last
// redundant load from `mapSet`. It used to ask for the bucket, get one whether
// the key was there or not, and then re-read `slots[at]` to find out which --
// a load, a bounds check and a representation test for an answer the probe had
// already computed and thrown away. A bucket is in `[0, mask]`, so `-bucket - 2`
// is at most -2 and can never be confused with an entry index or with the
// lookup miss. (`mask` would have to reach 2^31-1 for that subtraction to wrap,
// which is an index table of 8 GB.)
//
// Triangular offsets visit every bucket of a power-of-two table, and growth
// keeps an empty bucket available, so probing terminates. Advancing by
// 1, 2, 3, ... reduces primary clustering while retaining the compact
// insertion-order index table.
// The bucket hash. `hash(key)` is the key type's answer; a map that has caught
// itself clustering re-mixes it, and every map starts out trusting the key.
//
// **The branch is the cheap half of the trade.** It is one load and one
// perfectly-predicted test per probe, against the eight instructions of an
// unconditional avalanche mix — and against the locality that mix destroys,
// which is the larger number: `key_value_update`'s update phase is 7.25 ms with
// the mix applied to every key and 3.64 ms without it.
private fn mapHashOf<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>, key: K) -> Int {
    let h = hash(key)
    if (m.strong) { return keyStrengthen(h) }
    return h
}

private fn mapProbe<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>, key: K, findSlot: Bool) -> Int {
    let mut at = mapHashOf(m, key) & m.mask
    let mut step = 1
    loop {
        let entry = list_get(m.slots, at)
        if (entry < 0) {
            if (findSlot) { return 0 - at - 2 }
            return -1
        }
        let existing = list_get(m.keys, entry)
        if (eq(existing, key)) { return entry }
        at = (at + step) & m.mask
        step = step + 1
    }
}

// The insertion-order entry index of `key`, or -1.
public fn mapIndexOf<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>, key: K) -> Int {
    return mapProbe(m, key, false)
}

// Doubles the table and re-places every entry.
//
// **The slot list is grown in place rather than replaced**, and that is not a
// micro-optimisation: assigning a new `List` over `m.slots` releases nothing, so
// every rehash would leak the old table. Pushing the new half and then clearing
// the old half reuses the one allocation the map has ever had for this.
private fn mapRehash<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>) {
    let old = m.mask + 1
    let capacity = old * 2

    let mut i = old
    while (i < capacity) {
        list_push(m.slots, -1)
        i = i + 1
    }
    m.mask = capacity - 1

    // **The table checks the hash it is being given, and this is the whole
    // defence of a cheap `keyMixInt`.** Placing every entry into a table it has
    // just doubled costs about 0.2 displaced probes per entry when the hashes
    // spread; three per entry is not a run of bad luck at any size, it is a hash
    // that is not separating these keys. `i * 65537` under the fold in
    // std/key.psm is the family that does it — the two halves cancel and every
    // key lands in bucket 0 — and it reaches an average of three by the ninth
    // entry, so the wasted work before the switch is thirty-six probe steps.
    //
    // A map that switches never switches back: the keys that clustered are
    // still in it, and the strengthened hash is what is now keeping them apart.
    // A false positive costs that map its locality and nothing else, which is
    // why the threshold does not need to be delicate.
    if (mapPlaceAll(m) > list_len(m.keys) * 3 and m.strong == false) {
        m.strong = true
        mapPlaceAll(m)
    }
}

// Clear the index table and re-place every entry, answering with the total
// probe displacement — the number of buckets that were already taken.
//
// Existing entries are unique, so this needs an empty bucket and never a key
// comparison; that is work insertion already did.
private fn mapPlaceAll<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>) -> Int {
    let capacity = m.mask + 1
    let mut j = 0
    while (j < capacity) {
        list_set(m.slots, j, -1)
        j = j + 1
    }

    let n = list_len(m.keys)
    let mut displacement = 0
    let mut e = 0
    while (e < n) {
        let key = list_get(m.keys, e)
        let mut at = mapHashOf(m, key) & m.mask
        let mut step = 1
        while (list_get(m.slots, at) >= 0) {
            at = (at + step) & m.mask
            step = step + 1
            displacement = displacement + 1
        }
        list_set(m.slots, at, e)
        e = e + 1
    }
    return displacement
}

public fn mapHas<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>, key: K) -> Bool {
    return mapIndexOf(m, key) >= 0
}

// The value for `key`, or `None`.
//
// This is the lookup that can distinguish "absent" from "present and equal to
// the fallback", which mapGetOr cannot. It is what the earlier version of this
// file said would arrive with REQUIREMENTS 14, and it did.
public fn mapGet<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>, key: K) -> Option<V> {
    let at = mapIndexOf(m, key)
    if (at < 0) { return Option<V>.None }
    return Option.Some(list_get(m.values, at))
}

// Absence answered with a caller-supplied value. Kept alongside mapGet for the
// many callers that have a sensible default and do not want to match on one.
public fn mapGetOr<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>, key: K, fallback: V) -> V {
    let at = mapIndexOf(m, key)
    if (at < 0) { return fallback }
    return list_get(m.values, at)
}

// Update an existing value with one key lookup. The callback receives the old
// value and returns its replacement; it runs exactly once on a hit and never
// on a miss. Returns whether the key existed. The update itself does not insert
// or reorder entries. Example: mapUpdate(counts, key, |value: Int| value + 1).
public fn mapUpdate<K: Key + Copy, V, F>(m: Map<K, V>, key: K, update: F) -> Bool {
    let at = mapIndexOf(m, key)
    if (at < 0) { return false }
    let value = update.call(list_get(m.values, at))
    list_set(m.values, at, value)
    return true
}

// Insert or overwrite. Answers whether the key was already present, which is the
// one bit a caller cannot recover afterwards without a second lookup.
//
// Grows above 1/2 load after insertion. A spare index bucket costs four bytes;
// keeping probe chains short is worth that space on lookup/update workloads.
// Keys and values remain dense, so lowering occupancy only grows the index.
// Comparing with capacity / 2 also avoids overflowing count * 4 at large sizes.
public fn mapSet<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>, key: K, value: V) -> Bool {
    let found = mapProbe(m, key, true)
    if (found >= 0) {
        list_set(m.values, found, value)
        return true
    }
    mapInsert(m, key, value, 0 - found - 2)
    return false
}

// The half of `mapSet` that runs when the key is new, kept in its own function
// so that the half that runs when it is not can be inlined into a caller's loop.
//
// **This is a codegen shape, not a refactor, and it is worth 1.5x on an update
// loop.** `mapSet` overwriting an existing key is a probe and one store; the
// insert path is two pushes, a copy, a slot write and a possible rehash. Inlined
// together they are one body far over any inliner's threshold, so
// `mapSet(m, k, mapGetOr(m, k, 0) + 1)` compiled to two out-of-line calls that
// hash the same key twice and probe the same bucket twice, with nothing able to
// see that they were the same key. Split, the hot half inlines and both probes
// land in the caller where the optimiser can look at them together.
private fn mapInsert<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>, key: K, value: V, at: Int) {
    // The key is **copied in**, not moved: a parameter is a borrow, so
    // `list_push(m.keys, key)` is "cannot move out of borrowed value", which is
    // exactly why an owned key needs the separate `Copy` bound. A
    // `sink` parameter pushed here would be worse than either -- codegen
    // transfers nothing, so the caller's scope exit and the list's release both
    // free it, and `--verify` reports "release of a pointer that is not live".
    //
    // The **value** is not copied, and that is the limitation below.
    let index = list_len(m.keys)
    list_push(m.keys, copyOf(key))
    list_push(m.values, value)
    list_set(m.slots, at, index)

    if ((index + 1) > (m.mask + 1) / 2) { mapRehash(m) }
}

// The key at a position, for iterating a map. Positions are insertion order and
// stay valid until the next mapSet that inserts.
public fn mapKeyAt<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>, index: Int) -> K {
    return list_get(m.keys, index)
}

public fn mapValueAt<K: Key + Copy, V>(m: Map<K, V>, index: Int) -> V {
    return list_get(m.values, index)
}
